<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	Hardened Runtime, not App Sandbox. This is the `dmg` target (direct
	distribution + notarization), not `mas`, so com.apple.security.app-sandbox
	and its per-resource exceptions do not apply and must not be added here by
	analogy with an App Sandbox guide — they would sandbox an app that was
	never built for it.

	These two are what V8/Node need to keep running under Hardened Runtime.
	Everything else Patchbay does needs no entitlement:

	  - Spawning uv, clang, codesign, `open` and run-bridge.sh. Hardened
	    Runtime constrains what gets loaded INTO this process, not what it
	    exec()s. A child gets its own signature and its own rules, which is
	    also why the CPython uv downloads can load unsigned wheels' .so files
	    in the venv: that happens in the python process, not in ours.
	  - Setting DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES for bridge mode. run-bridge.sh sets it
	    for the instrumented Cortex Control copy, and it is that copy's own
	    signature (ad-hoc, hardened runtime OFF, allow-dyld-environment-
	    variables) that decides whether dyld honours it. Patchbay does not
	    need allow-dyld-environment-variables — that entitlement is about
	    DYLD_* in OUR OWN launch environment, which nothing sets.
	  - Network access. uv downloads CPython; Hardened Runtime does not gate
	    the network (App Sandbox does).

	Deliberately NOT here: disable-library-validation. Patchbay declares no
	runtime `dependencies` and npmRebuild is false — there is no native
	addon loaded into the main process. Add it only if one ever appears, and
	prefer signing that addon with the same team over weakening this.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
